From cefa6d209c07c8eb11aeef35c847f6bf4635e1be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:25:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 24/24] lib/streamaggr: hold a single output inside its entry,
 and sum_samples_total's state with its value

Without shared state -- enable_windows off, the common configuration and ACG's
-- each output series cost four allocations: the aggrValues entry (72 bytes,
rounded to 80), a one-element slice for its outputs, and for sum_samples_total
the value and the shared state it points at. Nothing about them needs to be
separate.

aggrValues now keeps the one output of a single-output aggregator without shared
state in an inline array, and puts everything else behind a pointer to a slice
laid out blue then green, so the entry is 48 bytes and there is no separate
slice. sum_samples_total allocates a value together with the state it owns
when nothing else shares it; a green value still points at its blue value's
state. Without shared state there is no green half, exactly as before.

On ACG-shaped series with the production config, per output series:

  heap      360 -> 313 bytes  (-13%; -28% with the sharded map)
  objects   6.2 -> 4.2

BenchmarkAggregatorsPushACGShape medians 126 -> 114 ms (sum_samples_total) and
125 -> 110 ms (sum_samples), fewer allocations to make.

The one existing enable_windows test does not notice blue and green being
confused, so TestAggrValuesKeepBlueAndGreenApart checks the layout directly:
right lengths, distinct values, green sharing blue's state. Returning the blue
half for green fails it.
